;Match details

* '''Match 1: Diallo def. Tanaka.''' The Hammer's first points — and it came against the Bulldog, the last man standing beside him in the Cannon Club's final days. Two big men, two brawlers, no love lost: the match was fought in the corners and on the floor. Tanaka's chops and Diallo's hammer blows echoed through the small gymnasium. The finish: Tanaka went for the Bulldog Bomb at 10; Diallo blocked, and the Hammer Slam at 10:57 ended it. Diallo is 1-3; Tanaka falls to 1-3, and the two men who held the Cannon Club's line walked out in opposite directions. '''Crowd:''' Split — the crowd respects both wars. '''HAMMER''' chants, ironic and not.

* '''Match 2: Ishida def. Takagi.''' The Dragon against the Regular Army's veteran — the grudge of the block's honest men. Ishida, still rebuilding from the 8:56 Jusuf humiliation, took Takagi to the mat and made him work for everything; Takagi's chest was red from the exchange by the ten-minute mark. The finish: Ishida went for the Ishida Lock at 12 — Takagi powered toward the ropes, but the veteran rolled through, kept the hold, and dragged him back to center. The Dragon tapped at 13:12 — the Ishida Lock's first giant-killing of the tournament. Ishida is 2-2; Takagi falls to 3-1, his first loss of the G1. '''Crowd:''' Dueling '''TAKAGI''' / '''ISHIDA''' chants. '''THIS IS WRESTLING''' during the mat exchanges. STANDING OVATION for the tap — the veteran's redemption, live.

* '''Match 3: Jusuf def. Ono.''' The rebound. Two nights after the bump heard around Okaiken, the truth-teller faced the young lion — the IC champion against the World TV champion, contempt against belief. Ono threw everything with the pure heart the crowd loves him for; Jusuf treated none of it as worth acknowledging. The finish: the mosaraza kneebar at 9:21 — the same hold that made Ishida tap in 8:56 — and Ono, the believer, tapped. Jusuf is 2-2; Ono falls to 1-3. Jusuf walked out without acknowledging the crowd, the opponent, or the questions. '''Crowd:''' '''ONO''' chants throughout. Silence for the tap — the crowd does not know what to do with Jusuf winning again.

* '''Match 4: Taumafa def. Reyes.''' The giant's fourth straight. Reyes's lucha speed kept him moving for ten minutes — the Cobra Twist nearly turned the match at 2.9 — but there is no twisting out of a 200-kilo Vaiahi giant, and the giant powerbomb at 11:34 ended it. Taumafa is 4-0, tied atop the block — the family's G1 is still perfect. Reyes falls to 0-4, and the tag champion's singles tour is a losing one. '''Crowd:''' '''TAUMAFA''' chants. GASPS at the Cobra Twist near fall. ERUPTION for the powerbomb.

* '''Match 5: KAZUKI def. Kuroki.''' The main event — the Kuroshio reunion. KAZUKI and Kuroki came up as brothers in the black; the stable broke in 2017 and the chapter has been reopened twice since — Kuroki's closing match with Maeda, KAZUKI's win over Kuroki at Episode 7. This was their first meeting in a G1 ring, and it was fought with the warmth of brothers and the stakes of the block. Kuroki's strong style is the hardest test of KAZUKI's selling — every forearm real, every suplex stiff — and KAZUKI took all of it and kept flying. The finish: Kuroki went for the Black Iron Bomb at 15; KAZUKI slipped out, and the Twin Star Press at 16:48 ended it. Clean pin. KAZUKI helped Kuroki up — the bow of the Kuroshio, both hands — and the two walked out together. KAZUKI is 4-0, alone atop Block B. '''Crowd:''' '''KAZUKI''' and '''KUROSHIO''' chants. GASPS at the strong-style forearms. ERUPTION for the Twin Star Press. STANDING OVATION for the reunion bow.

